WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Peace be upon you, and welcome to the fifth video of Module Two of our fellowship.

2
00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:11.520
In this analytical session, we will experience a pivotal turning point that fundamentally transforms how we think as healthcare workers and educators.

3
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:18.420
Without lengthy introductions, let's dive straight in with a famous allegory that public health pioneers across the globe are raised on.

4
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.340
A story that shifts us from mere rapid reaction to a far deeper strategy.

5
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.720
Picture this scene with me:

6
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.540
A quiet day, standing beside the bank of a deep, roaring river rushing past like a rocket.

7
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.200
Suddenly, we hear a terrifying cry for help:

8
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.220
A person is drowning, battling the waves.

9
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.380
Naturally, driven by pure human chivalry...

10
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:37.680
...you throw yourself into the water...

11
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.560
...pull the man out, and administer first aid.

12
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.960
He barely catches his breath when another scream erupts from a second person drowning!

13
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.420
You dive in and rescue him.

14
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.920
Then a third, a fourth, a fifth, and a tenth!

15
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.560
The situation turns into an exhausting, grueling marathon.

16
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.280
Doctors and rescuers deplete all their energy on the edge of the river...

17
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.320
...just trying to pull out the drowning victims.

18
00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.840
This scene is precisely what happens in our hospitals and emergency rooms...

19
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.560
...when we remain trapped in reactive response mode.

20
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.400
Until a wise person steps forward and asks the brilliant question:

21
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.560
'Everyone, why are we busy merely fishing out these drowning victims?

22
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.600
Who is throwing them into the river upstream in the first place?

23
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.880
Why don't we walk upstream to see what is going on?'

24
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.240
And when they reached the upstream source, they discovered the catastrophe:

25
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.920
There was a broken bridge, and people were falling through it!

26
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.460
Instead of staying downstream endlessly soaking our clothes, drying off, and pulling victims out...

27
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.620
...the smarter move is to go upstream, hammer a few nails, and fix the bridge.

28
00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:28.340
In public health, this concept is called 'Upstream Thinking'.

29
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.760
It is the cornerstone separating the two greatest philosophies in healthcare:

30
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.480
Treatment and Prevention.

31
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.260
This brings us to Section One:

32
00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.040
What is the fundamental difference between them?

33
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.460
The comparison is crystal clear:

34
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.620
Treatment always occurs after the damage is done, symptoms have flared up, and the patient is suffering.

35
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.000
Then we intervene with medications or surgery.

36
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.200
This is a noble and necessary medical role, of course...

37
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.020
...yet it remains fundamentally reactive.

38
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.360
Prevention, however, is a proactive stride.

39
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.060
It eradicates the problem at its very source...

40
00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.640
...keeping the individual healthy and thriving in their own home.

41
00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.820
Take diabetes, for example:

42
00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.080
Treatment means waiting for blood sugar...

43
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.540
...to advance and then prescribing insulin and glycemic regulators.

44
00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.060
Prevention means going down into the community early on...

45
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.340
...modifying dietary habits regarding sweets...

46
00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.400
...and hydrogenated oils...

47
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.540
...and promoting physical activity...

48
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.140
...to safeguard the pancreas before it gives out.

49
00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.840
Treatment deals with illness as an established reality...

50
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.500
...while prevention refuses to let disease occur in the first place.

51
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:23.020
Prevention is not just a poetic proverb like 'an ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure.'

52
00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.140
Rather, it is a robust strategy resting upon four major pillars:

53
00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.180
1. It curtails disease burden and morbidity accumulation.

54
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.480
2. It enhances quality of life, allowing individuals to live productively and vigorously...

55
00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:32.660
...instead of spending their years in chronic pain.

56
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:37.060
3. It saves billions squandered on operating theaters, emergency admissions, and pharmaceuticals.

57
00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.220
A pinch of prevention saves fortunes.

58
00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.980
4. It builds a society possessing natural resilience to withstand any epidemic or crisis.

59
00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.900
To put this into operational practice...

60
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.540
...public health experts divided prevention into three core tiers:

61
00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.840
First line of defense: Primary Prevention.

62
00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.600
Here, we slam the door shut before disease ever touches the human body.

63
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.520
Such as immunization programs, promoting athletics, balanced nutrition...

64
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.440
...and awareness drives deterring youth from smoking their very first cigarette.

65
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.060
Here, we erect a genuine protective shield.

66
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:09.580
Second line of defense: Secondary Prevention.

67
00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.560
If disease manages to sneak in silently without apparent symptoms...

68
00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.340
...our objective is catching it in its earliest stages before it erupts into a crisis.

69
00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.000
Like routine blood pressure screenings to identify the silent killer before a stroke...

70
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.940
...mammography screenings for early detection of breast cancer...

71
00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:27.680
...and HbA1c tests to detect prediabetes and adjust course.

72
00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.400
Third line of defense: Tertiary Prevention.

73
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.480
If disease has manifested, settled, and become chronic, we still never raise the white flag.

74
00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.540
Our goal is preventing complications and rehabilitating the patient so they live with the highest dignity and function.

75
00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:42.040
Such as cardiac rehabilitation programs following open-heart surgeries...

76
00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.400
...rigorous diabetic monitoring to protect kidneys, vision, and limbs...

77
00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.200
...and psychosocial support groups helping patients adapt and integrate within the community.

78
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.760
And do you know who the real dynamo is across all three tiers?

79
00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.180
It is the health educator.

80
00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:58.540
In primary prevention, they educate the public on healthy lifestyle habits.

81
00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:03.420
In secondary prevention, they dispel fear and hesitation, encouraging timely screenings.

82
00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:08.640
In tertiary prevention, they stand beside chronic patients, simplifying regimens and fostering positive living.

83
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.160
The health educator is the catalytic engine translating blueprints into tangible reality.

84
00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:16.120
Now, how do we apply this to climate crises?

85
00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.760
Picture an oppressive, scorching heatwave approaching:

86
00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:25.760
The clinical path: sitting in emergency rooms waiting for seniors and builders to collapse from heat stroke so we can hook them up to IV drips and injections.

87
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.680
The preventive path: mobilizing two full days before the heatwave hits...

88
00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.040
...hitting the streets, teaching people to hydrate before thirst sets in...

89
00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.360
...mandating employers halt labor during peak solar hours...

90
00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.360
...and checking in on the elderly in their homes.

91
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.020
Undoubtedly, proactive mobilization saves hundreds of lives before illness strikes.

92
00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.560
Climate is not a pathogen you cure with an antibiotic course.

93
00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.080
Climate transforms the rules of the entire playing field.

94
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.500
Prevention is the ultimate weapon for adaptation and building civic resilience.

95
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.300
All of this is encapsulated within the Public Health Mindset.

96
00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.860
Let's test this mindset on childhood obesity:

97
00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.800
The traditional clinical mindset suggests:

98
00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.280
'Open weight-loss clinics and treat children one by one.'

99
00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.760
Important, but that is downstream after the damage is done...

100
00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.820
...when the child has already gained excessive weight and struggles to move.

101
00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.740
Meanwhile, the upstream mindset marches directly into schools:

102
00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.580
Bans chips and sugary sodas in school cafeterias...

103
00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.080
...increases physical education hours...

104
00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:14.180
...and coaches parents on preparing wholesome, cost-effective home meals.

105
00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:17.580
Here, we shield an entire generation from the trap of obesity and its chronic comorbidities.

106
00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.580
True public health leaders constantly keep three questions front of mind:

107
00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.240
1. How do we prevent the problem from emerging in the first place?

108
00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.220
2. How do we identify it early in its infancy?

109
00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:27.340
3. How do we mitigate its ramifications and complications across society?

110
00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:30.620
In summary: clinical care rescues individuals after they fall ill...

111
00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:34.520
...but resilient societies are forged through proactive upstream prevention and early detection.

112
00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:39.100
Your mandate as a health educator begins in the field long before illness knocks on people's doors.

113
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.800
In our next journey in Video Six, we will answer the decisive question:

114
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.740
How do we pinpoint the genuine health challenges...

115
00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:48.680
...confronting our community without projecting top-down assumptions?

116
00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:52.860
We will master the golden tool: Community Health Needs Assessment...

117
00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.920
...and listen directly to the authentic voice of the field.

118
00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:58.200
Before we conclude, we leave you with this practical exercise:

119
00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:04.680
Select a health condition—such as diabetes, smoking, childhood obesity, or heat stress:

120
00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:09.320
Identify in your notebook: what intervention constitutes primary prevention to stop it?

121
00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:13.000
What screening represents secondary prevention for early detection?

122
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.740
And what tertiary program mitigates its long-term complications?

123
00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.860
Thank you for watching, and see you in the next video!
